      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 3 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1 (water meters, R49). The fragment contains four technical/metrological data tables:\n\n1. Section 3.4: UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³/h, software and checksum 13 25667\n   - Nominal diameter DN: 20 mm\n   - Material of body: brass / plastic\n   - Permanent flowrate Q₃: 4 m³/h\n   - Minimum flowrate Q₁: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5 l/h (8 variants)\n   - Transitional flowrate Q₂: 40, 32, 25.6, 20.32, 16, 12.8, 10.16, 8 l/h (8 variants)\n   - Overload flowrate Q₄: 5 m³/h\n   - Ratio Q₃/Q₁: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500, 630, 800 (8 R values)\n   - Ratio Q₂/Q₁: 1.6\n   - Connection thread: G 1 B\n   - Construction length L: From 110 to 190 mm\n   - Pressure loss: 0.40 bar, class Δp40\n   - Battery: 2 or 3 pcs, Umin 2.7V, Umax 3.7V, life time up to 16 years\n\n2. Section 3.5: UWM DN15, Q₃=1.6 m³/h, software and checksum 13 21574\n   - DN: 15 mm\n   - Material: brass / plastic\n   - Q₃: 1.6 m³/h\n   - Q₁: 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4 l/h (4 variants)\n   - Q₂: 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24 l/h\n   - Q₄: 2 m³/h\n   - Q₃/Q₁: 125, 160, 200, 250\n   - Q₂/Q₁: 1.6\n   - Connection thread: G ¾ B, G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\n   - Construction length: From 110 to 190 mm\n   - Pressure loss: 0.63 bar, Δp63\n   - Battery: 1 pc li-battery, Umin 3V, Umax 3.6V, life up to 10 years\n\n3. Section 3.6: UWM DN15, Q₃=2.5 m³/h, software and checksum 13 21574\n   - DN: 15 mm\n   - Material: brass / plastic\n   - Q₃: 2.5 m³/h\n   - Q₁: 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25 l/h (5 variants)\n   - Q₂: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10 l/h\n   - Q₄: 3.125 m³/h\n   - Q₃/Q₁: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400\n   - Q₂/Q₁: 1.6\n   - Connection thread: G ¾ B, G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\n   - Construction length: From 110 to 190 mm\n   - Pressure loss: 0.63 bar, Δp63\n   - Battery: 1 pc li-battery, Umin 3V, Umax 3.6V, life up to 10 years\n\n4. Section 3.7: UWM DN20, Q₃=2.5 m³/h, software and checksum 13 22681\n   - DN: 20 mm\n   - Material: brass / plastic\n   - Q₃: 2.5 m³/h\n   - Q₁: 20, 15.625, 12.5, 10 l/h (4 variants)\n   - Q₂: 32, 25, 20, 16 l/h\n   - Q₄: 3.125 m³/h\n   - Q₃/Q₁: 125, 160, 200, 250\n   - Q₂/Q₁: 1.6\n   - Connection thread: G 1 B\n   - Construction length: From 110 to 190 mm\n   - Pressure loss: 0.63 bar, Δp63\n   - Battery: 1 pc li-battery, Umin 3V, Umax 3.6V, life up to 10 years\n\nNow, how to model this in the layered schema?\n\nThe certificate covers multiple variants of the UWM water meter. Within each table, the Q₃/Q₁ ratio varies (i.e., Q₁ and Q₂ vary per R value). So within each of the four table groups (which I'd call model variants distinguished by DN + Q₃ + software/checksum), there are sub-variants distinguished by R ratio.\n\nApproach:\n- `certificate.number`: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" — the schema wants `number`. The revision history might track rev 1, but I don't have a revision_history in this fragment. I'll put number as \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\". Hmm, actually the full shown is \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". The schema says certificate.number — probably \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" is best since that's what appears on each page. Let me include it as shown. Actually, maybe I should keep it exactly as displayed: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". Since the fragment doesn't include the header page, this is the best info.\n\n- `certified_type.category`: \"Water meter\"\n- `certified_type.type_designations`: [\"UWM\"] — also the DN/Q3 variants. Maybe include model variants in model_family.\n\n- `model_family`: family_name \"UWM\", models with model_id per table group:\n  - \"UWM DN20 Q3=4 m³/h (13 25667)\" — hmm, model_id should be something canonical. Let me define model_id as e.g. \"DN20_Q3=4\" or \"UWM DN20 Q3=4\". The section headers give: \"UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³, software and checksum 13 25667\". So model_id could be \"DN20 Q3=4\" and attributes: nominal_diameter: 20, permanent_flowrate_q3: 4, software_checksum: \"13 25667\".\n\nActually the Q3 value \"4 m³\" in header but table says 4 m³/h. Use m³/h.\n\nAlso within each model, R ratio variants — those are configurations. I could model the R variants as configuration axis \"ratio_q3_over_q1\" with config_level entries.\n\nThe schema says:\n- `characteristics.model_level[]`: attribute × model matrix — values per model.\n- `characteristics.config_level[]`: attribute × config-axis matrix — values per condition.\n\nSo:\n- type_level: attributes constant across the whole cert: material of body (\"brass / plastic\"), ratio Q₂/Q₁ = 1.6, construction length 110–190 mm. Wait — construction length \"From 110 to 190\" is same across all four tables, so it can go in type_level. Pressure loss differs (0.40 for DN20 Q3=4; 0.63 for others), so model_level. Connection thread differs: DN20 → G 1 B; DN15 → G ¾ B or G 7/8 B - G ¾ B. That's model_level (thread depends on DN). Battery differs: 3.4 has 2 or 3 pcs batteries, 2.7–3.7V, 16 years; others 1 pc li-battery 3–3.6V, 10 years. Model level.\n\nActually, since connection thread has options (\"G ¾ B\" and \"G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\" meaning either/or?), value could be a string \"G ¾ B\" / \"G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". Hmm. For DN15: \"G ¾ B<br>G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\" — likely means thread options: G ¾ B, or G 7/8 B with G ¾ B... Actually in water meters, \"G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\" might mean adapter options. Keep as a string value.\n\nLet me structure model_level attributes:\n\nFor each attribute varying per model, values per model:\n\n1. `nominal_diameter_dn`: DN20→20, DN15→15, DN15→15, DN20→20. Varies per model — put in model_level.\n2. `permanent_flowrate_q3`: 4, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5 — model_level.\n3. `overload_flowrate_q4`: 5, 2, 3.125, 3.125 — model_level.\n4. `pressure_loss_max`: 0.40, 0.63, 0.63, 0.63 bar — model_level. Also pressure loss class Δp40/Δp63 — could be a separate attribute `pressure_loss_class` with values \"Δp40\", \"Δp63\".\n5. `connection_thread`: \"G 1 B\", \"G ¾ B\" / \"G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\", etc. — model_level.\n6. `battery`: battery_count 2-3 vs 1; voltage min 2.7 vs 3; voltage max 3.7 vs 3.6; life up to 16 vs 10 years. Model level attributes: `battery_quantity` ({min:2,max:3} vs 1), `battery_voltage_min` (2.7 V vs 3 V), `battery_voltage_max` (3.7 V vs 3.6 V), `battery_lifetime_max` (16 years vs 10 years). Also `battery_type`: for 3.4 it's not stated \"li-battery\" explicitly — just \"2 or 3 pcs\"; others \"1 pc li-battery\". Hmm, battery_type: null for first? I could put \"battery_description\" as a string. Let me be careful not to fabricate: 3.4 battery: \"2 or 3 pcs Umin 2,7V, Umax 3,7V, life time up to 16 years\". No type given. Others: \"1 pc li-battery Umin 3V, Umax 3,6V, life time up to 10 years\".\n\n7. `software_checksum`: \"13 25667\", \"13 21574\", \"13 21574\", \"13 22681\" — model_level attribute. Actually it's part of the model designation. I'll include as model attribute `software_checksum`.\n\n8. Q₁ min flowrate and Q₂ transitional flowrate and Q₃/Q₁ ratio vary WITHIN each model by R variant — that's config_level.\n\nConfig axis: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\" (R). For each model, values per condition:\n\nModel \"DN20 Q3=4\": R conditions 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500, 630, 800 with Q₁ 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5 and Q₂ 40, 32, 25.6, 20.32, 16, 12.8, 10.16, 8.\n\nBut config_level values take {condition, value}. Each attribute is a separate config_level entry, so:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"ratio_q3_over_q1\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": {\"model\": \"DN20 Q3=4\", \"R\": 160}, \"value\": {\"value\": 25, \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\"}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the schema says config_level values follow `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. Actually rule 5 says: `characteristics.config_level` with `axis`. Values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value}`. Let me define:\n\n```json\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n    \"axis\": \"ratio_q3_over_q1\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20 Q3=4 / R160\", \"value\": {\"value\": 25, \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\"}},\n      ...\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nHmm — but mixing model and R into condition strings is clunky but workable. Alternatively, since each model has its own R variants, maybe I should treat each model as its own matrix_table with rows per R value, plus model_level attributes across the 4 models.\n\nActually, maybe better: the four table groups are the \"models\" of the family, and within each model, R is a configuration axis. The R variants could also be considered sub-models... The schema example for matrix_tables uses rows per size (model). Here within each table, rows aren't rows — the columns across are variants. The table structure: rows = characteristics, columns = variants (R values). So each of the 4 tables is a matrix.\n\nI think a good representation:\n- `matrix_tables[]`: 4 entries, one per section (3.4–3.7), with name like \"Technical and metrological data UWM DN20, Q3=4 m³/h, software and checksum 13 25667\", columns = [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"R160\", \"R200\", ...] and rows as objects keyed by characteristic. Or transpose: columns [\"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1_l_h\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2_l_h\"] with one row per R.\n\nTransposing into rows per R is cleaner:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"UWM DN20 Q3=4 m³/h (software and checksum 13 25667)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"l/h\", \"l/h\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"ratio_q3_over_q1\": 160, \"minimum_flowrate_q1\": 25, \"transitional_flowrate_q2\": 40},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nAnd single-value characteristics of each table go into... they'd be lost from matrix_tables but captured in model_level.\n\nAlternatively, keep the raw orientation: rows keyed by attribute. I think transposed rows-per-R is more usable and matches the \"rows (object per row)\" idea.\n\nAlso include static attributes in matrix_tables rows? Could include columns for the constant ones too, but that duplicates model_level. I'll include the varying columns plus maybe constants as description. Let me include all: nominal_diameter, q3, q4, ratio_q2_over_q1, etc. — too noisy. I'll keep matrix tables to the per-R varying data, and note the fixed data in description.\n\n- `model_family.models`: 4 entries:\n  - {\"model_id\": \"UWM DN20 Q3=4\", \"label\": \"UWM DN20, Q3=4 m³/h, software and checksum 13 25667\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter_dn\": 20, \"software_checksum\": \"13 25667\"}}\n  - etc.\n\nModel_id format: maybe \"DN20_Q3=4\". Let me use \"DN20 Q3=4 m³/h\"? Hmm, I'll use simple: \"UWM DN20 Q3=4\". Actually two models share DN20 and two share DN15 and two share Q3=2.5, so model_id must distinguish DN+Q3: \n- \"DN20 Q3=4\"\n- \"DN15 Q3=1.6\"\n- \"DN15 Q3=2.5\"\n- \"DN20 Q3=2.5\"\n\nGood, unique.\n\n- `characteristics.model_level[]`: entries for attributes that vary across the 4 models:\n  - nominal_diameter_dn (mm): 20, 15, 15, 20\n  - permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): 4, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5\n  - overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): 5, 2, 3.125, 3.125\n  - pressure_loss_max (bar): 0.4, 0.63, 0.63, 0.63\n  - pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\", \"Δp63\", \"Δp63\", \"Δp63\"\n  - connection_thread: \"G 1 B\", \"G ¾ B or G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\", same, \"G 1 B\"\n  - battery_quantity: {min:2,max:3} pcs vs 1 — value objects\n  - battery_voltage_min (V): 2.7, 3, 3, 3\n  - battery_voltage_max (V): 3.7, 3.6, 3.6, 3.6\n  - battery_lifetime_max (years): 16, 10, 10, 10\n  - battery_type: null?, \"li-battery\", ... Hmm. For DN20 Q3=4 no type stated. I could include battery_type with value \"li-battery\" for the three and omit for first. In a values array, one entry could be missing. I might just include \"battery_chemistry\": {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"value\": \"li-battery\"} etc. That's fine.\n  - software_checksum: \"13 25667\", \"13 21574\", \"13 21574\", \"13 22681\"\n\n- `characteristics.type_level[]`: constant across all: \n  - material_of_body: \"brass / plastic\"\n  - ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6\n  - construction_length (mm): {min:110, max:190}\n\n- config_level: minimum_flowrate_q1 and transitional_flowrate_q2 per model × R.\n\nThat's 8+4+5+4 = 21 conditions per attribute. Long but correct.\n\nAlternatively, put Q1/Q2/R into matrix_tables only (per model) and skip config_level. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere; and config-level is exactly attribute × config-axis. The combination model × axis — condition can encode both. I'll do both: matrix_tables for fidelity and config_level for the structured approach? That's duplicative but the instructions for R137 say extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_level. Here the varying-per-R attributes fit config_level. I'll do both to be safe, mirroring the R137 example.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe treat each (model, R) combination as a distinct... no, 21 sub-models is overkill. The R variants within a model are configuration options (customer chooses R ratio). config_level fits.\n\nLet me define config_level entries with condition strings like \"model=DN20 Q3=4; R=160\". Hmm, or nested condition object: {\"model\": \"...\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\": 160}. The schema shows values[]: {condition, value} — condition can be a string or object; schema example for R137 model_level uses {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}. For config_level, no explicit example of value shape. Rule 5: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`? It says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value}\". So each entry: condition (string), value (structured value object?), or plain number? To be safe: {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": {\"value\": 25, \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}}. Hmm, rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. So yes, nested structured values.\n\nWait, but the R137 model_level example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — plain number, not structured. Inconsistent with rule 2 but the example is authoritative for model_level. Hmm. Rule 2 says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". The R137 example contradicts by using bare numbers. I'll follow the example format for model_level since it's concrete and matches \"model_level: attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}\" from the schema YAML:\n\n```\nmodel_level[]:                    # attribute × model matrix\n    attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}\n```\n\nSo schema's model_level values are {model, value, footnote_markers[]} — plain value, unit at entry level. OK so for model_level I'll use plain values with unit_symbol at the entry level (schema shows \"unit\" — I'll use \"unit_symbol\" per rule examples; the YAML says `attribute, unit, values[]`. Hmm, \"unit\" vs \"unit_symbol\". The R137 example JSON uses \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\". I'll use unit_symbol).\n\nFor config_level, YAML: \n```\nconfig_level[]:                   # attribute × config-axis matrix\n    attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}\n```\nSo values: {condition, value}. I'll use structured value objects? The example doesn't show. To be consistent with model_level plain values, I could do {condition, value, unit_symbol}? Rule 5 says: \"use `characteristics.config_level` with `axis` describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`\" — wait let me re-read: \"Values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value}`.\" Actually rule 5: \"use `characteristics.config_level` with `axis` describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value}`.\"\n\nHmm, it just says {condition, value}. I'll use {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": {\"value\": 25, \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\"}}? Or plain numbers with unit at entry: {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": 25, \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\"}? \n\nGiven ambiguity, I'll go with: {\"condition\": \"DN20 Q3=4 / R160\", \"value\": 25, \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\"}. Hmm, but that deviates from structured-value rule... The condition encodes the model too.\n\nActually, maybe a cleaner interpretation: config_level entries could include a \"models\" scoping. Let me just make condition a composite string and value a structured object per rule 2. Like:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"ratio_q3_over_q1\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"l/h\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"model=DN20 Q3=4, ratio_q3_over_q1=160\", \"value\": 25},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm. I think I'll include unit at the config_level entry level and structured {condition, value} with plain numeric value. That matches YAML shape most closely. But rule 2's strict structured value... Rule 2's shape applies to type_level style values. The schema's own YAML overrides for matrices. I'll keep plain values in matrices (as R137 example does) with unit_symbol at entry level.\n\nDecision:\n- model_level entries: {\"attribute\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"values\": [{\"model\": ..., \"value\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": []}]} — footnote_markers only if present (omit if empty, or include empty? Example omits them). I'll omit when empty.\n- config_level entries: {\"attribute\": ..., \"axis\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"values\": [{\"condition\": ..., \"value\": ...}]}.\n\nFor condition, I'll use \"DN20 Q3=4 / R160\" style. Or maybe better to make condition object? Strings are safer for json parsing. Use string.\n\nNow matrix_tables: 4 tables. Each with name (from section heading), columns, column_units, rows.\n\nTable 3.4 (UWM DN20 Q3=4, checksum 13 25667):\ncolumns: [\"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"l/h\", \"l/h\"]\nrows (8):\nR160: Q1=25, Q2=40\nR200: Q1=20, Q2=32\nR250: Q1=16, Q2=25.6\nR315: Q1=12.7, Q2=20.32\nR400: Q1=10, Q2=16\nR500: Q1=8, Q2=12.8\nR630: Q1=6.35, Q2=10.16\nR800: Q1=5, Q2=8\n